2026-09-10 19:51  pglock.py e8521241 (new), dispatcher.py 4f6e6f1e   [BOSS item 2, 2026-09-10]
  THE THIRD LOCK, MADE VISIBLE. pgserver serialises EVERY postgres start and stop on this machine
  through one lockfile — a CLASS attribute at a fixed per-user path, shared across worktrees,
  checkouts, xdist workers and unrelated projects, with the pgdata path never entering the lock
  identity and no timeout on the wait (★, docs/ops/2026-09-09/PGSERVER-GLOBAL-LOCK.md). It cost one
  run 102 minutes of invisible waiting. box.lock.d and portal.lock.d do not know it exists, so the
  board can show a run "holding the box" that is stalled behind a process holding no project lock at
  all — board-reports-what-it-sent, third lock edition.
  READ-ONLY BY CONSTRUCTION, AND THE CONSTRAINT CHOSE THE MECHANISM.
    * lsof was the obvious probe and IT DOES NOT WORK HERE. Measured against a process genuinely
      holding the lock: lsof's lock field reports `l ` (no lock) for BOTH flock and POSIX lockf on
      this macOS. It lists who has the file OPEN, conflating the holder with everyone queued behind
      it — the one distinction that matters, reported backwards.
    * A non-blocking flock/lockf attempt ACQUIRES the lock when it is free. Forbidden: this daemon
      must never become the thing that serialises a test run.
    * fcntl(F_GETLK) TESTS for a conflicting lock, never takes one, and returns the holder's pid.
      Verified both ways — HELD by the exact pid while a real holder ran, FREE the moment it exited,
      and an INDEPENDENT process still could not take the lock after our probes.
  THREE STATES: LIVE / STALLED / UNMEASURED. STALLED needs BOTH halves — the global lock held by a
  process that is not this run, AND effectively no CPU burned between two consecutive samples. One
  sample is never a stall. The run that HOLDS the lock is never STALLED: it is what everyone else is
  waiting for, a different row and a different action. And the wording says STALLED is a
  CORRELATION, not proof — a run idle for its own reasons looks the same.
  UNMEASURED IS NEVER RENDERED AS FREE. A missing lockfile, an unreadable one or a refused query all
  report UNMEASURED and say so on the board. platformdirs is NOT importable by /usr/bin/python3 — the
  daemon's interpreter — so the path is written out rather than derived, and a path that ever goes
  stale reports "no lockfile, NOT the same as a free lock" instead of a silent FREE.
  A dead pid's CPU is None, never 0.0: 0.0 is exactly the stall signature and would report every
  finished run as stalled forever.
  PROOF: tests/test_pglock.py (20 checks, new, wired) — the holder cases take a REAL POSIX lock in a
  REAL subprocess against a temp file, because a stubbed lock proves the parser and not the probe,
  which is the trap this detector already fell into once. Plus 4 call-site checks in
  test_sessionwatch.py (68 checks, was 64). Whole suite ALL GREEN.
  Seven mutations, 7/7 CAUGHT, every file restored to pre-image md5 — including the forbidden
  implementation (acquire instead of test), a missing lockfile reading as FREE, calling the holder
  stalled, dropping the CPU half, accepting one sample, a dead pid reading 0.0, and DELETING THE
  DAEMON'S PROBE, which scored MISSED until a check drove poll_sessions.
  NOT TOUCHED, and deliberately: the lock itself, pgserver, and platform/testsupport/. That is
  WORKER-1's lane.
  dispatcher/ has no CI. This local green is the only evidence that will ever exist.

